  "nonogramGuideIntro": "Nonograms are logic puzzles with simple rules and challenging solutions. The goal is to reveal hidden pictures by filling cells based on numbers on the side of the grid.",
  "nonogramGuideMethodDivisionDesc": "If joining two filled pieces would make a block bigger than the largest number, the square between them must be empty. Mark it with X to keep them apart.",
  "nonogramGuideMethodDivisionTitle": "5. Division",
  "nonogramGuideMethodDoublePositioningDesc": "Sometimes a filled square can belong to only one or two of the numbers. Try each possibility: squares that stay empty in every one of them get an X.",
  "nonogramGuideMethodDoublePositioningTitle": "6. Double positioning",
  "nonogramGuideMethodInaccessibilityDesc": "Squares that no block can reach are empty. Try each block at its furthest positions around the filled squares; anything outside that range gets an X.",
  "nonogramGuideMethodInaccessibilityTitle": "3. Inaccessibility",
  "nonogramGuideMethodNotFitDesc": "A gap between X's (or between an X and the edge) that is shorter than every number that could go there can't hold a block: mark it with X.",
  "nonogramGuideMethodNotFitTitle": "4. Doesn't fit in",
  "nonogramGuideMethodSuperpositionMultiDesc": "With several numbers, push the whole group left (keeping one empty square between blocks), then right. Where the same block covers a square in both positions, that square is filled. Quick check: add up the numbers and the gaps; what's left over is how far each block can move.",
  "nonogramGuideMethodSuperpositionMultiTitle": "Superposition (Multiple Clues)",
  "nonogramGuideMethodSuperpositionSimpleDesc": "If a number is more than half its line, part of it is always filled. Imagine the block pushed all the way left, then all the way right: the squares it covers both times are filled for sure. Example: 7 in 10 squares always fills the middle 4.",
  "nonogramGuideMethodSuperpositionSimpleTitle": "Superposition (Single Clue)",
  "nonogramGuideMethodWallPushingDesc": "A filled square near the edge limits where its block can be. The block can't go past the edge, so it must extend away from it. Example: 5 with the 4th square filled: even pushed against the left edge, the block still covers the 5th square.",
  "nonogramGuideMethodWallPushingTitle": "2. Pushing off from the walls",
  "nonogramGuideRuleDesc": "Each number represents one continuous block of filled cells in that row or column. If there are multiple numbers, they represent separate blocks, and each block must be separated from the next by at least one empty cell. For example, 3 1 means a block of 3 filled cells, then at least 1 empty cell, then a block of 1 filled cell.",
  "nonogramGuideRuleTitle": "Core rule: what the numbers mean",
  "nonogramGuideTitle": "How to Solve",
